	<item>
		<title>By: EdBoyWW</title>
		<link>http://fffff.at/compiz-is-the-most-important-thing-to-ever-happen-to-linux/comment-page-1/#comment-211</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[EdBoyWW]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 28 Dec 2007 15:10:55 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://fffff.at/compiz-is-the-most-important-thing-to-ever-happen-to-linux#comment-211</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[&quot;The kids don’t want stability, they want an OS w/ rims that spin.&quot;

...until they realize, that the beautiful task switcher and the mad spinning cube have actually no use.

If all the developers start to pimp the sh_t out of Compiz, we end up with an OS that brings all the eye candy and left alone with its old and useless technologies.

Compiz do have a major part in turning the people to Linux, but it is also extremely important that they want to solve their problems, they want to live with their OS.

If they are able to do so, there comes the eye candy.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>&#8220;The kids don’t want stability, they want an OS w/ rims that spin.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8230;until they realize, that the beautiful task switcher and the mad spinning cube have actually no use.</p>
<p>If all the developers start to pimp the sh_t out of Compiz, we end up with an OS that brings all the eye candy and left alone with its old and useless technologies.</p>
<p>Compiz do have a major part in turning the people to Linux, but it is also extremely important that they want to solve their problems, they want to live with their OS.</p>
<p>If they are able to do so, there comes the eye candy.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
